# The gate for reports that die before they print (2026-10-04)

A shell script running `set -e` that writes `name="$(pipeline)"` takes the pipeline's exit
status as the assignment's own status. When that status is non-zero the script ends at that
line. For a command that genuinely failed that is the right thing. It is wrong when the
non-zero status is a normal answer: `grep` exits 1 when nothing matched, `diff` exits 1 when
the files differ, `wc` exits non-zero when the file it was asked to measure is not there. In
those three cases the script has already decided what the non-zero answer means, and the
lines that say so sit below the assignment and never run.

`scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py` rejects that shape. This transcript records what it
finds on this tree, what it found in the three scripts that shipped it, the false positive
that fixed the rule's scope, the two bugs the gate itself shipped the same day, and the
container run. Output is quoted verbatim, including its own punctuation.

== 1. The three sites that shipped, driven from git

The three fixed files are taken at `021b5453^`, i.e. before the fix, and the gate is pointed
at that directory. Nothing about the tree under test is modified:

$ t="$(mktemp -d)" && mkdir -p "$t/scripts/ci"
$ for f in scripts/ci/check-versions.sh scripts/install.sh scripts/verify-in-docker.sh; do git show "021b5453^:$f" > "$t/$f"; done
$ python3 scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py --root "$t"
    scripts/ci/check-versions.sh:91: declared_names="$(grep -v '^$' <<<"$declared" | cut -d' ' -f1 | sort)"
        grep: exits 1 when nothing matched
        fix: the non-zero status here is a normal answer, and a plain assignment hands it to `set -e`; use a command without that status (`sed '/^$/d'`, `awk 'NF { n += 1 } END { print n + 0 }'`) or absorb it explicitly with `|| true` so the handling below stays reachable
    scripts/install.sh:306: size="$(wc -c < "$dest" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '[:space:]')"
        wc: exits non-zero when its input cannot be read
        fix: the non-zero status here is a normal answer, and a plain assignment hands it to `set -e`; use a command without that status (`sed '/^$/d'`, `awk 'NF { n += 1 } END { print n + 0 }'`) or absorb it explicitly with `|| true` so the handling below stays reachable
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh:244: moved="$(diff "${tree_before}" "${tree_after}" | sed -n 's/^[<>] [0-9][0-9]* [0-9][0-9]* //p' | sort -u)"
        diff: exits 1 when the files differ
        fix: the non-zero status here is a normal answer, and a plain assignment hands it to `set -e`; use a command without that status (`sed '/^$/d'`, `awk 'NF { n += 1 } END { print n + 0 }'`) or absorb it explicitly with `|| true` so the handling below stays reachable
    pipefail-report: 3 assignment(s) that can end an errexit script before it prints its report
$ echo "EXIT=$?"
    EXIT=1

The same three files as they are now are clean, which is the control that makes the run
above mean something rather than being a scanner that reports whatever it is shown:

$ h="$(mktemp -d)" && mkdir -p "$h/scripts/ci"
$ for f in scripts/ci/check-versions.sh scripts/install.sh scripts/verify-in-docker.sh; do cp "$f" "$h/$f"; done
$ python3 scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py --root "$h"
    pipefail-report: OK (3 shell script(s), 3 with `set -e` scanned, 0 hazard assignment(s))
$ echo "EXIT=$?"
