== 3. The false positive that decided the scope

The rule's first hit was a fourth file, `scripts/verify-gates.sh:455`:

$ grep -n 'set -' scripts/verify-gates.sh | head -3
    43:set -uo pipefail
$ sed -n '455p' scripts/verify-gates.sh
      lines="$(printf '%s\n' "${real_list}" | grep -c .)"

That line is the `--only` pattern check in the host gate runner. The runner sets `-uo
pipefail` and never turns `-e` on, deliberately: it aggregates every gate's failure into one
summary instead of dying on the first one. So the assignment's status has nobody to hand it
to, and the `not ok` branch below it does run. Both spellings were measured rather than
reasoned about:

$ bash -c 'set -uo pipefail; lines="$(printf "%s\n" "" | grep -c .)"; echo "reached the next statement, lines=$lines"'
    reached the next statement, lines=0
$ echo "EXIT=$?"
    EXIT=0
$ bash -c 'set -euo pipefail; lines="$(printf "%s\n" "" | grep -c .)"; echo "reached the next statement, lines=$lines"'
$ echo "EXIT=$?"
    EXIT=1

`pipefail` is what makes the status non-zero; `-e` is what ends the script. A rule keyed on
`pipefail` would therefore have flagged a line whose report works, and the fix I had already
made to it was reverted (`git checkout -- scripts/verify-gates.sh`, then `cmp` against
HEAD). The scope is `-e`, and the mistake is kept as a test rather than deleted: that exact
line is `OUT_OF_SCOPE_LINE` in `scripts/ci/test-check-pipefail-report.py`, asserted clean
under `set -uo pipefail` and flagged the moment `-e` is added, plus
`test_scope_follows_the_shells_own_errexit`, which runs both preludes through `bash -c` and
asserts whether the following statement is reached. Mutation M11 widens the scope pattern
back to "any flag cluster" and is killed by those two cases.

== 4. The gate's own first bug: an OK that had compared nothing

`STRICT_RE` decides whether a file is in scope. Its first revision was written without
`re.MULTILINE`, so its `^` could only match a script whose very first bytes were `set`.
Every file looked out of scope. Reproduced by deleting that one argument from the shipped
file, running the gate, and restoring with a plain `cp` followed by `cmp`:

$ orig="$(mktemp)" && cp scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py "$orig"
$ sed -i '/STRICT_RE/ s/, re.MULTILINE//' scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py
$ python3 scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py
    pipefail-report: OK (31 shell script(s), 0 with `set -e` scanned, 0 hazard assignment(s))
$ echo "EXIT=$?"
    EXIT=0
$ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-pipefail-report.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 17 tests in 1.213s

    FAILED (failures=13)
$ cp "$orig" scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py && cmp "$orig" scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py && echo restored
    restored

Exit 0, a green verdict, and a scanned range of nothing. The wording when this was first
noticed was `OK (31 shell script(s), 0 of them strict)`; it is the same empty range either
way. Two things close it: the verdict line prints the scanned count, and
`test_repository_is_clean_and_actually_scanned` parses that count out of the real verdict
and fails below 5. That is also why M1 ("strict pattern anchored to line 1") is the
strongest mutation in section 6: it kills 13 of the 17 cases.

== 5. The gate's own second bug: finding the end of a substitution by searching

To read the body of `name="$(...)"` the scanner has to find the paren that closes it.
Searching for the two characters `)"` does not work, because the closing quote of
`name="$(f)"` is what makes the substitution's last two characters `)"`; stripping quoted
contents first deletes the very sequence being searched for, and the body then runs to the
end of the file. That revision reported 12 findings on a tree that had none. Captured at the
time, before the parser was rewritten (this revision was never committed, so the run itself
cannot be repeated):

    scripts/l10n-guard.sh:217: sorted="$(printf '%s\n' "$listing" | sort -u)"
        rg exits non-zero as a normal answer, and the assignment hands that status to `set -e`
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh:591: used="$(fresh_token)"
        grep exits non-zero as a normal answer, and the assignment hands that status to `set -e`
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh:996: start="$(date +%s)"
        grep exits non-zero as a normal answer, and the assignment hands that status to `set -e`
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh:130: tree_dir="$(mktemp -d)"
        grep exits non-zero as a normal answer, and the assignment hands that status to `set -e`
    pipefail-report: 12 assignment(s) that can end a strict script

Four of the twelve are quoted above. Two of them, `tree_dir="$(mktemp -d)"` and
`start="$(date +%s)"`, contain no hazard command at all, which is what an over-run body
looks like: a stage from some later line gets attributed to an innocent assignment, and the
reported reason ("rg exits non-zero as a normal answer") names a command that is not in the
quoted line.

The fix is `walk()`, a context stack of `sub` / `dq` / `sq` frames, because neither
searching nor counting parens can find that close: `ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"
has a nested `$(` and a `(` inside quotes, and quote-stripping deletes the answer. The
durable reproductions are the two parsing fixtures, and they are checked by swapping the
shipped finder for each naive strategy in turn, running the suite, then restoring with `cp`
and verifying with `cmp`. Both strategies are four lines at the end of
`substitution_body()`:

    def substitution_body(text, pos):          # naive strategy 1
        end = text.find(')"', pos)
        return text[pos:end] if end >= 0 else ""

    def substitution_body(text, pos):          # naive strategy 2
        stripped = re.sub(r"'[^']*'|\"[^\"]*\"", "", text, flags=re.S)
        end = stripped.find(')"', pos)         # offsets now belong to a different string
        return text[pos:end] if end >= 0 else ""

$ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-pipefail-report.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 17 tests in 1.321s

    FAILED (failures=1)
$ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-pipefail-report.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 17 tests in 1.296s

    FAILED (failures=12)

The two strategies fail differently, and reading which cases died is more informative than
the counts. Strategy 1 kills exactly one case,
`test_hazard_after_a_nested_substitution_is_still_seen`: searching for `)"` in the raw text
stops at the first `)` followed by a quote, which is the real end in most lines and the
wrong one as soon as a substitution is nested. Strategy 2 kills 12 failures across 11 cases,
and it is the one that takes the two quoting fixtures down with it:

    FAIL: test_absorption_inside_a_string_does_not_count
    FAIL: test_every_site_in_a_file_is_reported
    FAIL: test_finding_names_the_answer_and_the_remedy
    FAIL: test_hazard_after_a_nested_substitution_is_still_seen
    FAIL: test_here_document_body_is_not_scanned
    FAIL: test_measured_lines_are_all_flagged        (three subTests)
    FAIL: test_multiline_assignment_reported_at_its_first_line
    FAIL: test_nested_quoting_does_not_leak_past_the_assignment
    FAIL: test_paren_inside_a_quoted_string_does_not_end_the_body
    FAIL: test_script_without_errexit_is_out_of_scope

The live verdict stayed `OK` under both patches, which is recorded on purpose: an
over-running scanner and a stopping-early scanner print the same green line on a clean tree,
and only the fixtures tell them apart.

== 6. Fixtures and mutations

$ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-pipefail-report.py
    Ran 17 tests in 1.359s

    OK

The 17 cases are: the three measured lines each flagged at a known line number; every site
in a file reported (3 findings, not the first only); the finding text naming both the answer
and the remedy; a multi-line assignment reported at its first line; a hazard after a nested
substitution still seen; a paren inside a quoted string not ending the body; nested quoting
not leaking past the assignment; `|| true` absorbed; `local`/`export` out of scope; a `set
-uo pipefail` script out of scope until `-e` is added; the errexit boundary measured against
`bash -c` itself; a non-hazard pipeline accepted; command names inside strings not treated
as stages; `|| true` inside a string not counted as absorption; here-document bodies not
scanned (paired with a positive control); the repository clean and provably scanned; and an
empty tree reported as an error rather than as a pass.

Mutations, each applied to the shipped file, the fixture suite run, the file restored with
`cp` and verified with `cmp`. Two things stop a mutation from being counted as killed for
the wrong reason: the unmutated baseline is run first and a non-green baseline aborts the
battery, and the count that decides the verdict is the `FAIL:` and `ERROR:` lines of
unittest's output, so a mutation that breaks the file so badly that no test runs scores 0
and is printed as SURVIVED with the last line of output beside it (a traceback, in that
case, rather than a verdict). A mutation whose anchor does not appear exactly once is not
applied and is likewise reported as SURVIVED.

    baseline (unmutated gate): 'OK', failures=0

    KILLED  13  M1 strict pattern anchored to line 1 (the bug shipped today)
    KILLED   2  M2 quoted text left visible to the stage scan
    KILLED   2  M3 explicit `|| true` no longer absorbed
    KILLED   1  M4 a quote inside a substitution never opens a string
    KILLED   8  M10 first stage of the substitution never scanned
    KILLED   2  M5 wc dropped from the hazard list
    KILLED   1  M6 here-document bodies scanned as code
    KILLED   1  M7 assignment pattern unanchored (local/export matched too)
    KILLED   1  M8 only the first site per file reported
    KILLED   6  M9 line number off by one
    KILLED   2  M11 scope widened to any flag cluster (brings the verify-gates.sh false positive back)

    mutations: 11  survived: 0
    gate restored byte-identical

M1 is the empty-range bug from section 4 and kills 13 of the 17 cases. M4 in this matrix
replaces the `else: stack.append("dq")` branch of the string-frame logic; the more obvious
mutation, dropping `stack.append("sub")`, was tried first and turned out to be an equivalent
mutant for the inputs then in the suite, which is why M10 and two of the parsing cases above
exist.

== 7. Wiring

The gate runs in CI and in the container entry point, on executable lines in both:

$ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (61 files in scripts/ci/, 60 reachable, 56 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)
$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --list | grep -c ''
    35
$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --list | grep -n 'pipefail'
    17:pipefail report
$ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test 2>&1 | tail -2

    verify-gates self-test: 47/47 cases pass

`check-guard-wiring.py` is what proves the two wirings are real rather than prose: it parses
the `gates=()` array of `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` and the `run:` blocks of
`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, and counts `scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py` and
`scripts/ci/test-check-pipefail-report.py` among the 56 reachable from the container runner.
The host runner's `--list` shows the label at position 17 of 35, and `--self-test` still
parses this repository's real gate array as one of its 47 cases.

