The `10 path(s) differ from HEAD` line is the runner's own honesty check and is quoted on
purpose: this run describes the working tree, not a commit, because the gate and its
fixtures were still uncommitted when it ran. The image was built from
`docker/verify.Dockerfile` in the same command (every layer cached, build context 788 B), so
the container read the live tree through the bind mount. `1 of 33` is the unfiltered array;
`cargo test` and `GUI protocol types` are appended by `--full` and are not among the 33.

The same command was re-run later the same day over a `git clone` of this repository checked
out at `11cdd8a2`, so that the run had a commit to be attributed to and nothing else. The line
this section had to explain is then simply not printed:

$ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "pipefail report"
    == source tree: 4011 files, checksum 3878427692
    == --only is in effect: 1 of 33 gates selected, this is not a sweep
    ...
    selected gates passed in chaos-verify:frozen
      --only was in effect: 1 of 33 gates ran, so this is not a full sweep

Only the four lines carrying the point are quoted; the fixture leg between them is the same
17 tests as above. That run also left one root-owned path in the clone, and it was not a
Python cache: `./target`, empty, created by the runtime as the mount point of
`chaos-verify-target` inside the bind mount. The gate under test here is not exposed to
that, because its fixtures run the check as a subprocess rather than importing it. The whole
of it, including the host-side `mkdir` that removes it, is written up in
`docs/verification/container-hygiene-2026-10-04.log`.

== 9. The sweep, and the one gate that has to run last

The docs for a change cite its evidence log by path, and `check-doc-path-refs.py` rejects a
doc that names a file the repository does not have yet. So the doc pass and the sweep have a
fixed order: writing the log is what makes that gate green. Moving the log out of the way
puts the sweep back into the state this change was in an hour earlier, and the gate fails on
exactly the citations that were written first:

$ mv docs/verification/pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log /tmp/held-pipefail.log
$ python3 scripts/ci/check-doc-path-refs.py; echo "exit=$?"
    check-doc-path-refs: CHANGELOG.md:29: `docs/verification/pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log` resolves to nothing in the repository (unrecorded)
    check-doc-path-refs: CHANGELOG.md:60: `docs/verification/pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log` resolves to nothing in the repository (unrecorded)
    check-doc-path-refs: 275 Markdown documents scanned against 4006 tracked paths, 50 dangling mention(s), 26 recorded; 2 problem(s)
    exit=1
$ mv /tmp/held-pipefail.log docs/verification/pipefail-report-gate-2026-10-04.log

The first full sweep taken mid-change failed on this one gate for this one reason, 30 other
labels green and the log still unwritten. With everything in place, the host sweep over the
whole gate list is green:

$ d="$(mktemp -d)"; bash scripts/verify-gates.sh > "$d/sweep.log" 2>&1; echo "exit=$?"
    exit=0
$ grep -c '^PASS' "$d/sweep.log"
    31
$ grep -E '^(FAIL|SKIP)|pipefail|all gates' "$d/sweep.log"
    PASS  pipefail report
    SKIP  cargo check (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  cargo clippy (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  cargo test (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  GUI protocol types (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    all gates passed on the host (31 run, 4 skipped)

31 of the 35 labels run on the host; the four skipped ones are the build gates, which
`scripts/verify-gates.sh` leaves to `--with-build` and which the container runs for real in
`docs/verification/verify-in-docker-full-021b5453-2026-10-04.log`. The `pipefail report` line
above is the new label running against this working tree at its position 17 of 35.

== 10. Why this entry point had only ever been run from a throwaway clone

The run above took 15 seconds. Before today it could not be run from a working tree at all,
because the repository had no `.dockerignore` and `docker build` therefore uploaded the
entire context for a Dockerfile that reads exactly one file:

$ du -sh target .git apps/chaos-ui/node_modules
    270G    target
    210M    .git
    143M    apps/chaos-ui/node_modules
$ du -sh --exclude=target .
    890M    .
$ wc -c < rust-toolchain.toml
    740

`docker/verify.Dockerfile` has one `COPY`, of `rust-toolchain.toml`, and the source tree is
bind-mounted at `/src` when the container runs, with cargo output in named volumes. So the
upload was pure waste. `.dockerignore` now carries `*` plus a single `!rust-toolchain.toml`.
Measured with a two-line probe Dockerfile that copies exactly what the real one copies:

$ d="$(mktemp -d)"
$ printf 'FROM scratch\nCOPY rust-toolchain.toml /\n' > "$d/ok.Dockerfile"
$ docker build --progress=plain -f "$d/ok.Dockerfile" -t chaos-ctx-probe:tmp .
    #1 transferring dockerfile: 80B done
    #2 transferring context: 1.23kB done
    #3 transferring context: 41B done
    #5 naming to docker.io/library/chaos-ctx-probe:tmp done
$ t0=$(date +%s%3N); docker build -q -f "$d/ok.Dockerfile" -t chaos-ctx-probe:tmp . >/dev/null; t1=$(date +%s%3N)
$ echo "context-to-image: $((t1 - t0)) ms"
    context-to-image: 303 ms

The first `transferring context` line is the `.dockerignore` itself; the second is the build
context, 41 bytes of checksums rather than the tree, because BuildKit pulls only what a
`COPY` needs. The real image build transfers 788 B at that step, which is the 740-byte
toolchain pin arriving; that command is the last one in the reproduce list.

An allow list is only safe if forgetting a file fails loudly, so the negative control is
part of the evidence. The same probe, copying something the ignore file does not allow:

$ printf 'FROM scratch\nCOPY CHANGELOG.md /\n' > "$d/bad.Dockerfile"
$ docker build --progress=plain -f "$d/bad.Dockerfile" -t chaos-ctx-probe:bad .
    ERROR: failed to build: failed to solve: failed to compute cache key: failed to calculate checksum of ref cni70ams834isst161p23r9e3::q8cvtbjgd6n2aoxoqhtb1eppp: "/CHANGELOG.md": not found

The build fails at build time rather than silently producing an image with a missing file,
which is the property the allow list needs. The probe image and Dockerfiles were removed
afterwards.

== 11. What the fix is not

`scripts/verify-gates.sh` was not changed and is byte-identical to HEAD; section 3 says why.
The gate does not model `set +e` regions, so a file that turns `-e` on anywhere is scanned
even if the hazard sits inside a `set +e` block; no such site exists on this tree, so
nothing depends on that being wrong either way. The `local x="$(cmd)"` class (ShellCheck
SC2155) is out of scope by design: the declaration masks the status instead of propagating
it, so its failure mode is an empty value used later, which is a different bug with a
different fix, and no such site has been observed to break here.
`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` deliberately has no `--list` of its own; it points at
`scripts/verify-gates.sh --list`, which prints the labels from the same array.

== 12. Reproduce it

    # the live tree, and the range it covered
    python3 scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py --verbose

    # the three shipped sites, pulled from git
    t="$(mktemp -d)" && mkdir -p "$t/scripts/ci"
    for f in scripts/ci/check-versions.sh scripts/install.sh scripts/verify-in-docker.sh; do
      git show "021b5453^:$f" > "$t/$f"
    done
    python3 scripts/ci/check-pipefail-report.py --root "$t"

    # the fixtures, and the errexit boundary measured against bash itself
    python3 scripts/ci/test-check-pipefail-report.py
    bash -c 'set -uo pipefail; lines="$(printf "%s\n" "" | grep -c .)"; echo "reached, lines=$lines"'
    bash -c 'set -euo pipefail; lines="$(printf "%s\n" "" | grep -c .)"; echo "reached, lines=$lines"'

    # in the container, one gate
    IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "pipefail report"

    # the build context the container entry point uploads
    docker build --progress=plain -f docker/verify.Dockerfile -t chaos-verify:ctx . 2>&1 | grep 'transferring context'
